{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016NqktsLgvvNYtmedhBbTiF","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# WI-286 — worker/integrator worktrees run the ambient interpreter, not the venv\n2\t\n3\t**WI:** WI-286 · **Status:** queued · **Delivers:** the unattended worktree\n4\tlaunch env (harness layer; sibling of WI-274) — no new spine SR\n5\t\n6\t## Problem\n7\t\n8\tThe dispatcher runs each train in its own git worktree\n9\t(`C:\\Projects\\ai-template-trains\\<train>`) and each worker session shells out to\n10\trun the harness (pytest, `check.py`, the generators). The Python those commands\n11\tresolve is whatever is **ambient on the session's PATH** — nothing points a\n12\tworktree session at the repo's `./.venv`. In run `20260723T0202` every worker\n13\tinherited the launch shell's interpreter (**Python 3.8**, below the declared 3.11\n14\tfloor); it happened not to break because 3.8 passed most tests, but:\n15\t\n16\t- it is **below the WI-262/WI-270 floor** the repo declares and CI enforces, so a\n17\t  3.11-only idiom would pass locally in a worktree and only fail in CI;\n18\t- a worktree has **no `.venv`** of its own, so a bare `python -m pytest` there\n19\t  resolves to a system interpreter that may lack the pinned dev tools\n20\t  (`requirements-dev.txt`) — the pytest-cov/xdist the bar assumes;\n21\t- it compounds WI-285: once the integrator bar actually runs, it must run under a\n22\t  floor-satisfying, pinned interpreter or it re-imports this fragility.\n23\t\n24\tThis is the walk-away-run twin of WI-274 part A (which fixes the *primary*\n25\tcheckout's stale venv): the same \"which Python runs the harness\" question, at the\n26\tper-worktree boundary the dispatcher owns.\n27\t\n28\t## Approach\n29\t\n30\t- **Point worktree sessions at a floor-satisfying, pinned interpreter.** Options\n31\t  to weigh in the build (record the choice):\n32\t  - have the dispatcher pass the primary repo's `./.venv` interpreter into the\n33\t    worktree session env (e.g. prepend `<root>/.venv/Scripts` to PATH, or set the\n34\t    harness `{py}` to an absolute venv path) — the worktrees share the root venv,\n35\t    no per-worktree install;\n36\t  - or create/reuse a per-worktree venv (heavier; likely unnecessary since the\n37\t    kit is stdlib-only and only the *tests* need the pinned tools).\n38\t    Lean: share the root venv by absolute path — one pinned toolchain, zero\n39\t    per-train install cost.\n40\t- **Preflight the interpreter floor** at worker/integrator launch: refuse (or\n41\t  loudly warn + record) when the resolved harness Python is < 3.11, so a\n42\t  below-floor run never silently produces a green.\n43\t- Tests: a worktree session resolves the intended interpreter; a sub-3.11\n44\t  resolution is caught at preflight (mirrors WI-274's discovery tests).\n45\t\n46\t## Interfaces\n47\t\n48\tNo cross-module seam — intra-module (`agent_dispatch` worktree/session env +\n49\tpreflight, and tests). Sibling scope: WI-274 (dev-setup/venv) and WI-285\n50\t(integrator bar) share the \"which Python runs the harness\" concern.\n51\t\n52\t## Done-when\n53\t\n54\t- [x] A worker/integrator worktree session resolves a ≥ 3.11, pinned-tool\n55\t      interpreter for the harness (verified: the run does not silently use\n56\t      ambient 3.8). `_activate_root_venv` points the dispatcher and every child it\n57\t      spawns (worker agent sessions, the pre-commit floor, `./scripts/check.sh`)\n58\t      at the ROOT `.venv` by absolute path; `_run_combined_bar`'s `{py}` resolves\n59\t      through `harness_python(root)` to the same `.venv`\n60\t      (`test_combined_bar_runs_under_the_root_venv_interpreter`,\n61\t      `test_activate_root_venv_points_children_at_the_pinned_toolchain`).\n62\t- [x] A non-pinned harness interpreter is caught at preflight, not after a false\n63\t      green: `_harness_floor_failures` rides the dispatcher's preflight gate and\n64\t      refuses before any worker is spawned or the integrator bar runs — a\n65\t      **missing/incomplete root `.venv`** (fail-closed, never a fall-back to\n66\t      ambient — REVIEW-A), a below-floor `.venv`, and an unrunnable `.venv` all\n67\t      fail closed (`test_harness_floor_*`, and end-to-end\n68\t      `test_dispatcher_refuses_a_below_floor_harness_interpreter` +\n69\t      `test_dispatcher_refuses_a_venvless_root`).\n70\t- [x] Decision recorded: **shared root `.venv` by absolute path** (not per-worktree)\n71\t      — see Resolution; downstream/template disposition noted there.\n72\t- [ ] Commit bar green; row `done`, spec archived. _(row-flip + archive are the\n73\t      integrator's close-ritual at done-flip; the worker ran the smoke + full\n74\t      suite — pasted in the session log.)_\n75\t\n76\t## Resolution\n77\t\n78\t**Shared root `.venv`, not a per-worktree venv.** The lean the spec preferred is\n79\twhat shipped: the kit is stdlib-only, so only the *tests* need the pinned tools\n80\t(`requirements-dev.txt`), and creating a venv per train would pay a real install\n81\tcost per dispatch for zero benefit. Instead the dispatcher **shares the root\n82\t`.venv` by absolute path**. Three coordinated moves, all in the dispatcher (the\n83\tone process that owns the primary checkout and leases the worktrees):\n84\t\n85\t- `agent_common.venv_python(root)` / `harness_python(root)` resolve the repo's\n86\t  own `.venv` interpreter (probing both `bin/python` and `Scripts/python.exe`).\n87\t- `agent_dispatch._activate_root_venv(root)` prepends that `.venv`'s bin dir to\n88\t  the dispatcher's own `PATH` (and sets `VIRTUAL_ENV`) once at launch, so every\n89\t  child inherits it — the worker's agent `python -m pytest`, the pre-commit floor\n90\t  (whose `$REPO_ROOT/.venv` probe misses in a venv-less worktree and falls through\n91\t  to `PATH`), the integrator's staging commits, and `./scripts/check.sh`.\n92\t- `_run_combined_bar`'s `{py}` resolves through `harness_python(root)` too, so the\n93\t  composed-tree bar runs the `.venv` even when the dispatcher was itself launched\n94\t  on ambient Python (closing the ambient-3.8 hole WI-285's `{py}=sys.executable`\n95\t  would otherwise re-import — the interaction WI-285 flagged).\n96\t\n97\t**Preflight, fail-closed.** `_harness_floor_failures(root)` folds into the same\n98\tpreflight failure list `dispatch_run` already gates on (joined with `+`, so no\n99\tnew branch touches `dispatch_run`'s complexity baseline). It refuses on three\n100\tshapes, all of which mean the harness would NOT run under the pinned root `.venv`:\n101\t\n102\t- **no runnable root `.venv` at all** — absent, or a present-but-incomplete/corrupt\n103\t  layout (`venv_python` finds no interpreter). This fails closed and does **not**\n104\t  fall back to the ambient interpreter (REVIEW-A MAJOR): an ambient Python can\n105\t  clear the version floor yet lack the pinned `requirements-dev.txt` tools\n106\t  (pytest-cov/xdist the bar assumes), so a green worker run there is a *false*\n107\t  green — the very thing this WI exists to prevent. Points at `dev-setup\n108\t  --install` (WI-274) to create `./.venv`;\n109\t- a below-floor `.venv` (`< MIN_PYTHON`, 3.11);\n110\t- a `.venv` whose interpreter cannot be run to report a version.\n111\t\n112\tThe check targets the **harness** interpreter (the root `.venv`), not the\n113\tdispatcher's own orchestration interpreter, so a stdlib-only dispatcher launched\n114\ton an older Python with a proper 3.11 `.venv` still passes — the venv is what runs\n115\tthe tests. (An earlier build accepted a missing `.venv` whenever the ambient\n116\tinterpreter was `≥3.11`; REVIEW-A flagged that as re-opening the false-green hole,\n117\tsince `harness_python` then silently selected ambient `sys.executable`. The gate\n118\tnow accepts only the `.venv`.)\n119\t\n120\t**Why the floor lives in the dispatcher, not the shared `preflight()`.** A worker\n121\tprocess runs `agent_loop.py` under the dispatcher's own interpreter (which may be\n122\tambient) while its *harness children* resolve the injected `.venv`; checking the\n123\tworker's own `sys.version_info` in the shared `preflight()` would false-refuse a\n124\tworker whose harness is in fact floor-satisfying. The dispatcher gates every\n125\tworker launch and injects the venv they use, so validating once there — at\n126\t\"integrator launch\", which also gates worker launch — is both correct and\n127\tsufficient.\n128\t\n129\t**Template / downstream.** No new schema or migration. `agent_dispatch.py` /\n130\t`agent_common.py` are kit scripts that ship downstream verbatim, so an adopter\n131\tgets this fix on re-sync with no action; the setup scripts already create `.venv`\n132\tat the 3.11 floor. `MIN_PYTHON` mirrors the `(3, 11)` the setup scripts hardcode;\n133\tan adopter deliberately targeting an older product runtime lowers it alongside\n134\t`setup.{sh,ps1}`'s own check (noted at the constant).\n135\t\n136\t**Known residue (not this WI).** The dispatcher's own orchestration interpreter is\n137\tstill whatever the launcher resolves (bare `python`); WI-274 owns pointing the\n138\tlauncher at the venv. The floor here guarantees only that the *harness* runs\n139\tfloor-satisfying, which is the false-green surface this WI targets.\n140\t\n141\t## Notes\n142\t\n143\t- Filed 2026-07-23 from the run-babysit. Consider building alongside WI-274\n144\t  (same env concern, different boundary) and before/with WI-285 (the integrator\n145\t  bar needs the right interpreter to be meaningful).\n146\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f5aa2a62-2c8f-42f6-91ae-46b8aac033ce","uuid":"78b9a846-6987-4f1f-b538-dc1910a1b7c6","timestamp":"2026-07-24T12:00:20.544Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template-trains\\p0-g3-WI-286-9253\\docs\\specs\\WI-286.md","content":"# WI-286 — worker/integrator worktrees run the ambient interpreter, not the venv\n\n**WI:** WI-286 · **Status:** queued · **Delivers:** the unattended worktree\nlaunch env (harness layer; sibling of WI-274) — no new spine SR\n\n## Problem\n\nThe dispatcher runs each train in its own git worktree\n(`C:\\Projects\\ai-template-trains\\<train>`) and each worker session shells out to\nrun the harness (pytest, `check.py`, the generators). The Python those commands\nresolve is whatever is **ambient on the session's PATH** — nothing points a\nworktree session at the repo's `./.venv`. In run `20260723T0202` every worker\ninherited the launch shell's interpreter (**Python 3.8**, below the declared 3.11\nfloor); it happened not to break because 3.8 passed most tests, but:\n\n- it is **below the WI-262/WI-270 floor** the repo declares and CI enforces, so a\n  3.11-only idiom would pass locally in a worktree and only fail in CI;\n- a worktree has **no `.venv`** of its own, so a bare `python -m pytest` there\n  resolves to a system interpreter that may lack the pinned dev tools\n  (`requirements-dev.txt`) — the pytest-cov/xdist the bar assumes;\n- it compounds WI-285: once the integrator bar actually runs, it must run under a\n  floor-satisfying, pinned interpreter or it re-imports this fragility.\n\nThis is the walk-away-run twin of WI-274 part A (which fixes the *primary*\ncheckout's stale venv): the same \"which Python runs the harness\" question, at the\nper-worktree boundary the dispatcher owns.\n\n## Approach\n\n- **Point worktree sessions at a floor-satisfying, pinned interpreter.** Options\n  to weigh in the build (record the choice):\n  - have the dispatcher pass the primary repo's `./.venv` interpreter into the\n    worktree session env (e.g. prepend `<root>/.venv/Scripts` to PATH, or set the\n    harness `{py}` to an absolute venv path) — the worktrees share the root venv,\n    no per-worktree install;\n  - or create/reuse a per-worktree venv (heavier; likely unnecessary since the\n    kit is stdlib-only and only the *tests* need the pinned tools).\n    Lean: share the root venv by absolute path — one pinned toolchain, zero\n    per-train install cost.\n- **Preflight the interpreter floor** at worker/integrator launch: refuse (or\n  loudly warn + record) when the resolved harness Python is < 3.11, so a\n  below-floor run never silently produces a green.\n- Tests: a worktree session resolves the intended interpreter; a sub-3.11\n  resolution is caught at preflight (mirrors WI-274's discovery tests).\n\n## Interfaces\n\nNo cross-module seam — intra-module (`agent_dispatch` worktree/session env +\npreflight, and tests). Sibling scope: WI-274 (dev-setup/venv) and WI-285\n(integrator bar) share the \"which Python runs the harness\" concern.\n\n## Done-when\n\n- [x] A worker/integrator worktree session resolves a ≥ 3.11, pinned-tool\n      interpreter for the harness (verified: the run does not silently use\n      ambient 3.8). `_activate_root_venv` points the dispatcher and every child it\n      spawns (worker agent sessions, the pre-commit floor, `./scripts/check.sh`)\n      at the ROOT `.venv` by absolute path; `_run_combined_bar`'s `{py}` resolves\n      through `harness_python(root)` to the same `.venv`\n      (`test_combined_bar_runs_under_the_root_venv_interpreter`,\n      `test_activate_root_venv_points_children_at_the_pinned_toolchain`).\n- [x] A non-pinned harness interpreter is caught at preflight, not after a false\n      green: `_harness_floor_failures` rides the dispatcher's preflight gate and\n      refuses before any worker is spawned or the integrator bar runs — a\n      **missing/incomplete root `.venv`** (fail-closed, never a fall-back to\n      ambient — REVIEW-A), a below-floor `.venv`, and an unrunnable `.venv` all\n      fail closed (`test_harness_floor_*`, and end-to-end\n      `test_dispatcher_refuses_a_below_floor_harness_interpreter` +\n      `test_dispatcher_refuses_a_venvless_root`).\n- [x] Decision recorded: **shared root `.venv` by absolute path** (not per-worktree)\n      — see Resolution; downstream/template disposition noted there.\n- [ ] Commit bar green; row `done`, spec archived. _(row-flip + archive are the\n      integrator's close-ritual at done-flip; the worker ran the smoke + full\n      suite — pasted in the session log.)_\n\n## Resolution\n\n**Shared root `.venv`, not a per-worktree venv.** The lean the spec preferred is\nwhat shipped: the kit is stdlib-only, so only the *tests* need the pinned tools\n(`requirements-dev.txt`), and creating a venv per train would pay a real install\ncost per dispatch for zero benefit. Instead the dispatcher **shares the root\n`.venv` by absolute path**. Three coordinated moves, all in the dispatcher (the\none process that owns the primary checkout and leases the worktrees):\n\n- `agent_common.venv_python(root)` / `harness_python(root)` resolve the repo's\n  own `.venv` interpreter (probing both `bin/python` and `Scripts/python.exe`).\n- `agent_dispatch._activate_root_venv(root)` prepends that `.venv`'s bin dir to\n  the dispatcher's own `PATH` (and sets `VIRTUAL_ENV`) once at launch, so every\n  child inherits it — the worker's agent `python -m pytest`, the pre-commit floor\n  (whose `$REPO_ROOT/.venv` probe misses in a venv-less worktree and falls through\n  to `PATH`), the integrator's staging commits, and `./scripts/check.sh`.\n- `_run_combined_bar`'s `{py}` resolves through `harness_python(root)` too, so the\n  composed-tree bar runs the `.venv` even when the dispatcher was itself launched\n  on ambient Python (closing the ambient-3.8 hole WI-285's `{py}=sys.executable`\n  would otherwise re-import — the interaction WI-285 flagged).\n\n**Preflight, fail-closed.** `_harness_floor_failures(root)` folds into the same\npreflight failure list `dispatch_run` already gates on (joined with `+`, so no\nnew branch touches `dispatch_run`'s complexity baseline). It refuses on three\nshapes, all of which mean the harness would NOT run under the pinned root `.venv`:\n\n- **no runnable root `.venv` at all** — absent, or a present-but-incomplete/corrupt\n  layout (`venv_python` finds no interpreter). This fails closed and does **not**\n  fall back to the ambient interpreter (REVIEW-A MAJOR): an ambient Python can\n  clear the version floor yet lack the pinned `requirements-dev.txt` tools\n  (pytest-cov/xdist the bar assumes), so a green worker run there is a *false*\n  green — the very thing this WI exists to prevent. Points at `dev-setup\n  --install` (WI-274) to create `./.venv`;\n- a below-floor `.venv` (`< MIN_PYTHON`, 3.11);\n- a `.venv` whose interpreter cannot be run to report a version.\n\nThe check targets the **harness** interpreter (the root `.venv`), not the\ndispatcher's own orchestration interpreter, so a stdlib-only dispatcher launched\non an older Python with a proper 3.11 `.venv` still passes — the venv is what runs\nthe tests. (An earlier build accepted a missing `.venv` whenever the ambient\ninterpreter was `≥3.11`; REVIEW-A flagged that as re-opening the false-green hole,\nsince `harness_python` then silently selected ambient `sys.executable`. The gate\nnow accepts only the `.venv`.)\n\n**Why the floor lives in the dispatcher, not the shared `preflight()`.** A worker\nprocess runs `agent_loop.py` under the dispatcher's own interpreter (which may be\nambient) while its *harness children* resolve the injected `.venv`; checking the\nworker's own `sys.version_info` in the shared `preflight()` would false-refuse a\nworker whose harness is in fact floor-satisfying. The dispatcher gates every\nworker launch and injects the venv they use, so validating once there — at\n\"integrator launch\", which also gates worker launch — is both correct and\nsufficient.\n\n**Template / downstream.** No new schema or migration. `agent_dispatch.py` /\n`agent_common.py` are kit scripts that ship downstream verbatim, so an adopter\ngets this fix on re-sync with no action; the setup scripts already create `.venv`\nat the 3.11 floor. `MIN_PYTHON` mirrors the `(3, 11)` the setup scripts hardcode;\nan adopter deliberately targeting an older product runtime lowers it alongside\n`setup.{sh,ps1}`'s own check (noted at the constant).\n\n**Known residue (not this WI).** The dispatcher's own orchestration interpreter is\nstill whatever the launcher resolves (bare `python`); WI-274 owns pointing the\nlauncher at the venv. The floor here guarantees only that the *harness* runs\nfloor-satisfying, which is the false-green surface this WI targets.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Filed 2026-07-23 from the run-babysit. Consider building alongside WI-274\n  (same env concern, different boundary) and before/with WI-285 (the integrator\n  bar needs the right interpreter to be meaningful).\n","numLines":146,"startLine":1,"totalLines":146}}}
